<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walter Bagehot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1826">1826</a>–<a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walter Bagehot was an English journalist, businessman and essayist who wrote on government, economics, literature and race. Born in Langport, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, he founded the National Review with Richard Holt Hutton in 1855 and from 1861 edited The Economist for 16 years, widening its reporting of politics. The English Constitution (1867) examined Parliament and the monarchy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> Street (1873) gave the dictum that central banks in a crisis should lend freely to solvent institutions against sound collateral at high rates, often cited after the 2008 financial crisis. His Physics and Politics (1872) also developed a form of pseudoscientific racism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walter-bagehot.jpg" alt="Walter Bagehot" width="330" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Norman Hirst (1862–1956), after Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walter_Bagehot_NPG_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In The English Constitution (1867) <b>Walter Bagehot</b> named three rights left to the sovereign: “the right to be consulted, the right to encourage, the right to warn”.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Walter Bagehot</b>, in The English Constitution of 1867, called this close union, “the nearly complete fusion, of the executive and legislative powers”, the “efficient secret” of the British system, and contrasted the “dignified” parts of the constitution, such as the Crown, with the “efficient” cabinet that actually governs.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Walter Bagehot</b> complained that the American system offered no way to replace a failed leader in a crisis, and that an executive denied the laws it needed, facing a legislature without responsibility, left each blaming the other; Woodrow Wilson asked how the nation, like a schoolmaster, was “to…</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Weaknesses</a></li></ul></section>
